- [boat vs Aptible: agent sandboxes vs an app platform](https://boat.dev/compare/boat-vs-aptible): Aptible hosts apps and databases in Docker containers on AWS. boat gives each agent a full VM with Docker, KVM, a desktop and a disk saved every minute.
- [boat vs Google Agent Platform Sandbox: full VMs, not containers](https://boat.dev/compare/boat-vs-vertex-computer-use): boat vs Google's Agent Platform sandboxes: a full VM with root, Docker, /dev/kvm and a 60fps desktop, against unprivileged containers for code, shell and a browser.
- [boat vs Cloudflare Workers for Platforms: VMs vs isolates](https://boat.dev/compare/boat-vs-cloudflare-workers-for-platforms): boat vs Cloudflare Workers for Platforms: full Linux VMs with a disk, Docker and coding agents, against V8 isolates that run your customers' code per request.
- [boat vs Unikraft Cloud: full agent VMs vs millisecond microVMs](https://boat.dev/compare/boat-vs-unikraft-cloud): boat vs Unikraft Cloud: full VMs with Docker, /dev/kvm, SSH and built-in coding agents, against Firecracker microVMs that boot in milliseconds and sleep when idle.
- [boat vs Mastra: a full VM for agents, not a framework](https://boat.dev/compare/boat-vs-mastra): Mastra is a TypeScript agent framework with a hosting platform and short-lived sandboxes. boat gives agents a full VM with Docker, a desktop and coding agents.
- [boat vs Datalayer: full Linux VMs vs Python notebook runtimes](https://boat.dev/compare/boat-vs-datalayer): boat vs Datalayer: a full Linux VM with root, Docker and coding agents built in, against Jupyter-based Python runtimes for agentic data analysis.
- [boat vs Expo: agent VMs that last, not build jobs](https://boat.dev/compare/boat-vs-expo): Expo's EAS runs mobile app builds and CI jobs on fresh VMs for up to 2 hours. boat gives agents a Linux VM that keeps its disk, runs Docker and hosts apps.
- [boat vs Archal: a real VM vs simulated SaaS APIs](https://boat.dev/compare/boat-vs-archal): boat gives an agent a full Linux VM with Docker, root and coding agents built in. Archal gives it fake, stateful copies of GitHub, Slack and Stripe to test on.
- [Build an Issue-to-PR Software Factory on boat](https://boat.dev/blog/software-factory): OpenFactory turns GitHub, Linear, and Sentry issues into planned, tested, independently reviewed pull requests. Learn how to deploy and customize the pipeline on boat.
- [Containers vs VMs: why your agent sandbox should be a real machine](https://boat.dev/blog/containers-vs-vms): AI agents run untrusted code, need Docker, and keep long-lived state. A look at where container sandboxes break down and why boat gives every agent a full VM.
- [Benchmarking Five Sandbox Providers with a Devin-Style Agent Build](https://boat.dev/blog/integration-benchmark): We had a coding agent build a Devin clone on boat, E2B, Daytona, exe, and Islo. Build costs, times, error rates, and which sandbox lifecycles actually work.

## Machine-readable files

- [Agent contract](https://boat.dev/auth.md): auth rules agents must follow
- [OpenAPI spec](https://boat.dev/openapi.yaml): public boat API surface (YAML)
- [Public plans](https://boat.dev/api/plans): plan prices, quotas, and concurrency ceilings (JSON, no auth)
- [Sitemap](https://boat.dev/sitemap.xml): every indexable page on this site

## Install the boat CLI

- macOS / Linux: `curl -fsSL https://boat.dev/install | sh`
- Windows: `irm https://boat.dev/install.ps1 | iex`
